---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { BOT_USER_AGENT } from '../lib/bot';
import { PAGE_CACHE_SECONDS } from '../lib/cache';
import '../styles/doc.css';

// Story 2.5: how Saleaway works, and (in "Our bot") what its bot is. No database query. The old `/contact` address, which the
// bot's user-agent carries, forwards to the "Our bot" part.
Astro.response.headers.set('Cache-Control', `public, max-age=${PAGE_CACHE_SECONDS}`);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How Saleaway works"
  description="How Saleaway finds South African online store sales, what the discount figures mean, what we keep and how our bot behaves."
  path="/about"
>
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="doc">
    <h1>How Saleaway works</h1>

    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>
      Saleaway lists South African online stores that have a sale on, with the biggest discount we found and when we last checked the store. It is an
      independent site: it is not run by, affiliated with or endorsed by any store listed. Every store link opens that store's own page in a new tab.
    </p>

    <h2>How the discounts are read</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Once a day, a program reads each store's sale page. It does this automatically, so nobody checks the figures by hand.</li>
      <li>"Up to 40% off" is the biggest discount we found on that page. "On sale" means the page shows a sale but we found no figure.</li>
      <li>"Checked today" or "Checked 2 days ago" says how fresh the information is. A store whose sale looks over, or whose check is too old, is not shown.</li>
      <li>A figure can be wrong or out of date. Check the store's page before you buy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we keep</h2>
    <p>
      Only the store's name, the address of its sale page, the discount figure and the times we checked. We do not copy pictures, logos or product details
      from any store.
    </p>

    <h2 id="our-bot">Our bot</h2>
    <p>
      The program that reads the sale pages is a bot. It identifies itself as <code>{BOT_USER_AGENT}</code>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        It asks each store for a few requests a day at most: its sale page, its <code>robots.txt</code> and, for some Shopify stores, their public product
        list.
      </li>
      <li>It obeys <code>robots.txt</code>.</li>
      <li>It does not try to hide who it is or to get round a store's protection.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A store that does not want to be read can say so in its <code>robots.txt</code>, and the bot will leave it alone. For example:</p>
    <pre>User-agent: SaleawayBot
Disallow: /</pre>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
